    "ls|LS17_S2_U_163": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n��������� �\n������������������������� �\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nANNUAL INCOME FOR TRIBAL COMMUNITIES \n����������������������������������� �\n���������������������������� �\nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Indian Council of Social Science Research, New Delhi has conducted a study on \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nWestern Ghats and the Eastern Ghats on generating annual inc ome for the tribal communities living \nin the vicinity of the sanctuary in Karnataka; \n(b) if so, details thereof; and \n(c) whether the Government would replicate the scheme in other States, including Vizianagaram and \nVisakhapatnam in Andhra Pradesh for the b enefit of Tribal communities and if so, the details \nthereof? \n������ �\n�������������������� ���������� �\n����������������� ��\n \n(a)�to (b)  : As per the information provided by the  Indian Council of Social Science \nResearch (ICSSR), New Delhi, they have not conducted s��������������������������������������������������������\nBilgiri Rangaswamy Temple Wildlife Sanctuary�� (BRTWLS)  located between Western \nGhats and the Eastern Ghats on generating annual income for the tri bal communities \nliving in the vicinity of the sanctuary in Karnataka.  However, I CSSR has awarded a \nResearch Project to Dr. M. Balasubramanian, Assistant Professor, Institute for Social and \nEconomic Change (ISEC), Bengaluru ��������������������������������������������������est Resources: A Case \n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������commenced with effect from 1-02-2018 \nand is an ongoing project. \n \nEconomic Value of Bilgiri Rangaswamy Temple Wildlife Sanctuary (BRTWLS), the \nWestern Ghats in Karnataka is the part of the study taken on Economic value of Forest \nResources. This study has estimated the value  of carbon sequestration, provisioning \nservices, soil erosion prevention and recreation services. It has used market price \nmethods for estimating the value of provisioning services and individual travel cost \nmethod for estimating the value of recreation se rvices, while the value of carbon \nsequestration and soil erosion prevention has been estimated based on the secondary data. \nAs per the study, a verage annual income from non -timber forest products has been \nbetween Rs. 10000 and 12000 for the sample households in the BRT wildlife sanctuary. \n \n(c)�There is no such proposal under consideration. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_164": "LOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 164 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nPipeline owned by GAIL \n \n†164. SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृतिक गैस मंत्री \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total kilometre pipeline in possession of Gas Authority of India Limited (GAIL) at present \nand whether GAIL proposes to expand its pipeline network;  \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to hive off GAIL’s pipeline business and allot it to any \nnew company, if so, the reasons therefor; \n  \n(c) whether GAIL is failing in running the pipeline business smoothly at present, if so, the \nreasons therefor; and  \n \n(d) whether GAIL is working on any pipeline project in Bihar and if so, the present status of the \nsaid project along with the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृतिक गैस मंत्री \n(श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) GAIL is currently operating about 12,200 km Natural Gas Pipeline Network and \nabout 2,000 km Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG) pipeline network in the country. At present, \nGAIL is implementing approx imately 6000 km. of pipeline projects including capacity \naugmentation of existing pipeline, replacement of old pipeline, customer connectivity \nprojects etc.  \n \n(b) The Government has been making concerted efforts to bring policy reforms aimed \ntowards market based gas pricing regime for domestic gas by linking it with the global gas \nmarkets as well as by providing marketing and pricing freedom to gas producers from \ndifficult fields to attract investment in E&P activities and enhance gas production. With a \n\nview to promote transparency and competition in the gas marketing, Government in -principle \nbelieves that gas transmission and marketing should be two separate functions and has been \ntaking appropriate decisions from time to time in this direction. \n \n(c) No, Sir.  \n \n(d) Section-1 of Jagdishpur Haldia Bokaro Dhamra Pipeline (JHBDPL), which is passing \nthrough Bihar (418 km) has been commissioned.  Part of Section -2B (Dobhi-Durgapur \npipeline) of JHBDPL (18 km)  and part of Barauni -Guwahati pipeline (276 km) , which are \nalso passing through Bihar are at various stages of implementation/commissioning. City Gas \nDistribution Network at Patna has also been commissioned.  \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_165": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 165 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT TO IT PROFESSIONALS \n \n165. SHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL:  \n           SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state : \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that most of the IT profes sionals do not have adequate skills to \nwork in the fields of Artificial Intelligence, Internet of Things, Big Data Analytics, etc.; \n \n(b) if so, the steps being taken by the Government to upgrade their skills; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to include these subjects as part of College \nCurriculum and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) whether the Government is planning to open trai ning facilities in these \nnew subjects and if so, the details of the training  centres being planned in Odisha and other \nStates in the current year? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI R. K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (b) The IT-BPM (Information Technology and B usiness Process Management) \nIndustry in India employs ~ 4.5 million people. As per the reports available in the market, \nabout 30% of the existing workforce would require r e-skilling/ upskilling to stay relevant and \nabout 50-60% of the workforce would require re-skil ling/ upskilling on continual basis in \nemerging technologies to retain the edge that India  has in the IT sector due to growing \nautomation, emergence of disruptive as well as new technologies, etc. \n \nTo align the IT industry of India with the dynamic world market, NASSCOM - a Sector \nSkill Council under aegis of Ministry of Skill Deve lopment and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) has \nlaunched FutureSkills platform on 19.02.2018. The p rogramme aims to re-skill 2 million \nprofessionals and potential employees & students in  the industry over a period of 5 years. \nFutureSkills digital platform is providing a space where a learner can access content on all \nthe skills of the future. A learner can seamlessly access free and paid content, assessments, \nvirtual labs and get certified on the skills of their choice. \n \n FutureSkills focuses on 155+ skills spanning acros s 70+ job roles on 10 emerging \ntechnologies namely Artificial Intelligence, Blockchain, Big Data Analytics, Cloud Computing, \nCyber Security, Internet of Things, Mobile Tech, Ro botic Process Automation, Virtual Reality \n& 3D Printing. As on date, FutureSkills has offered  training to approximately 6 lakh \ncandidates.  \n \nFurther, to reskill and upskill IT-professionals to  be adept with the emerging \ntechnologies National Skill Development Corporation  (NSDC) under the aegis of MSDE has \nestablished ‘Future of Work’ initiative. In associa tion with IT-ITeS Sector Skill Council \n(NASSCOM), NSDC has designed and developed Qualific ation Packs – National \nOccupational Standards across 9 emerging technologi es such as Cyber Security, Artificial \nIntelligence, Big Data Analytics, Cloud Computing, Block Chain, Robotic Process \nAutomation, Internet of Things, Virtual Reality, an d Social & Mobile Application. Further, \nMSDE is incentivizing training providers to take up  these courses for training under the Skill \nDevelopment Mission.  \n \nIn the areas of long term training, under the Craft sman Training Scheme at Industrial \nTraining Institutes, new courses were introduced in 2018, such as Smart Agriculture (Internet \nof Things), Smart Healthcare (Internet of Things), Smart City (Internet of Things), Remotely \nPiloted Aircraft, etc. \n \n(c) and (d) Ministry of Skill Development and Entre preneurship presently has no such \nplan to include these courses in college curriculum and establish training centers.  \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_166": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. 166 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.11.2019 \n \nRESTORATION OF MONUMENT IN HAMPI \n  \n \n \n  \n166.  SHRI  P.C. MOHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR CULTURE  AND TOURISM \n(PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the UNESCO World heritage   sites in \nHampi in Bellary district in Karnataka are developing cracks, as reported in \nthe media; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has any plans to restore the monuments; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not,  the reasons therefor? \n \n (a) \n  & \n (b) \n \n \n \n \n \nThe World Cultural Heritage sites in Hampi  are protected monuments of ASI \nfor which essential conservation, repair and maintenance work is attended \nregularly within the archaeological norms & principles and conservation \npolicy of the ASI. There are no such cracks. \n \n \nThe conservation work of protec ted monuments is a continuous process. \nThe essential conservation and maintenance work at protected monuments \nof Hampi is  attended regularly depending upon the need of repairs at \ndifferent sites and availability of resources and they in a good state of \npreservation \n      \n (c) \n  & \n (d) \nThe conservation work of protected monuments is a continuous process. \nThe essential conservation and maintenance work at protected monuments \nof Hampi is  attended  regularly depending upon the need of repairs at \ndifferent sites and availability of resources and they in a good state of \npreservation. \n      ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_167": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 167\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2019\n \n \n \nCONSERVATION OF CULTURAL IDENTITIES\n \n \n167.  SHRIMATI PRATIMA MONDAL:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the G\novernment is conducting any programme in order to \nconserve cultural identities in urban areas; \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the details thereof and the problems faced in conducting so; and \n \n(c)\n \nif not, the plans for the future?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (I/C) FOR CULTURE \nAND TOURISM\n \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL)\n \n \n(a) & (b)\n \nTo protect, promote and conserve various forms of folk art and \ncultural identities throughout the country, the Government of India has set up \nseven Zonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs) with headquarters at Patiala\n, Nagpur, Udaipur, \nPrayagraj, Kolkata, Dimapur and Thanjavur. These ZCCs organize various cultural \nactivities and programmes on regular basis all over India including urban areas.  \n \n \n \nApart from this, the Government of India has been organizing Rashtriya \nS\nanskriti Mahotsavs (RSMs) since 2015 at various places in the country with details \nas under:\n \n \ni.\n \nNew Delhi (November, 2015 and October, 2016)\n \nii.\n \nVaranasi (December, 2016) \n \niii.\n \nBengaluru & Tawang (March, 2017)\n \niv.\n \nGujarat (October, 2017)\n \nv.\n \nTehri (May, 20\n17)\n \nvi.\n \nKarnataka (January, 2018) \n \nvii.\n \nMadhya Pradesh (February, 2018 & October, 2019). \n \n \n \nNo major problems/\nconstraints have been faced by the Ministry so far in \nconducting such programmes.  \n \n \n(c)\n \nDoes not arise.\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_168": "����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *168 \n����������������������������������������������������������\n��\nTigers in Bandipur Forest \n��\n������������ ����������������������������������\n�� �� ��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������\n��\n�������� ���������������� �������������� ������ ������������������ �������������� �������� �������� �������������� ���������������������� ���������������� ������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n��\n��\n�������������������������������������������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\n��������\n\n��\n��\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a), (b), (c) & (d) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *168 ON TIGERS IN BANDIPUR \nFOREST DUE FOR REPLY ON 29.11.2019 \n \n��\n�������������������� �������� ������������������ ������������ ���������������� �������� ���������������������� ���������������� ������������ �������������������������� ����������\n�������� ������������ ������������������ �� �������������������� �������� ������������������ ������������ ���������������� ������ ���������� ������ ��������\n���������������������� ���������������� ������������ �������������������� ������������ ������������������ ������ �������� ���������������������� ��������\n������������������ �������������������������������� �������������� ������������ ������������������ ������ ���������������������� ��������������������\n�������� ������������������������������ ������������ ������������������ ������ ������������ ���������� �������� �������� ����������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ���������������� �� ������������ ���������������� ������ ���� �������������������� �������� ������������������ ������ ������������ ������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n�������������������� ������������ ������������������ ������������������ ������ ���� ���������������� ������ ������ ������������������ �������������������� ������ �������� ������������\n�������������������������� ���������� ������������������ ������������ ���������������� �������� ������ �������� ������������������ ��������������������������\n���������� ������������ �������������������� ������������ ���������������������� ���������������������������� �������� ���������������� ���������� ������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��\n��������\n��",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_169": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \n��������������������\n��������������������������������������������������\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2019 \n \n������������������\n \n*169. SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)��whether the Government has finally evolved a policy on Genetically Modified (GM) \ncrops; and \n(b)��if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n��������������\n��\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������\n \n(a) to (b) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n*** \n  \n\n��\n��\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� �������� ������ ������ ������������������ ������ ���������������������� �������������������� ������ ������������ ������ ����������\n����������������������������������������������\n \n \n(a) to (b) Genetically Modified (GM) Technology is one amongst several available \ntechnologies for crops    that has potential for yield enhancement, pest and \ndisease resistance etc. Department of Agriculture Cooperation and Farmers \nWelfare supports research and release of GM crops, if it is conducted as per \napproved procedures and guidelines and in conformity with various rules and \nenvironmental safety measures prescribed under the Environment \n(Protection) Act, 1986. \n \nIn India, there is a well-established regulatory framework for approval of \nGenetically Modified Organisms (GMOs) as per “Rules for the Manufacture / \nUse / Import/ Export and Storage of Hazardous Microorganisms, Genetically \nEngineered Organisms or Cells”, 1989 (Rules 1989) established under the \nEnvironment (Protection) Act, 1986.  \n \nEvaluation of each application of GMO is done on a case-to-case basis after a \nthorough examination of health, environment, food and feed safety \nassessment studies undertaken in a systematic and scientific manner as per \nprescribed guidelines, manuals and standard operating procedures stipulated \nby various regulatory agencies from time to time.  \n \nThe data generated by the applicants is reviewed at every step in the \ndevelopment process of Genetically Modified (GM) crops by various \nstatutory committees, such as Institutional Biosafety Committee (IBSC), \nReview Committee on Genetic Manipulation (RCGM) in Department of \nBiotechnology, Ministry of Science and Technology and Genetic Engineering \nAppraisal Committee (GEAC) in the Ministry of Environment, Forest and \nClimate Change.  \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_17": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  17 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 18th NOVEMBER, 2019/ KARTIKA 27, 1941(Saka) \nVANISHING COMPANIES \nQUESTION \n17: Shri B.N. Bache Gowda: \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS                    ���������� �� �� �������� �������� \nbe pleased to state:     \n(a)�  the details of number of companies included in the list of vanishing \ncompanies in the country at present; and \n(b)�  the details of action taken against such companies? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \nAND CORPORATE AFFAIRS              (SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n�������������������������� �� ��������������������������������������������������                    (���������������������������������� �� ��) \n(a) A Coordination and Monitoring Committee (CMC), co -chaired by \nSecretary, Ministry of Corporate Affairs and Chairman, SEBI was set up in \nthe year 1999 to look into the issue s of Vanishing Companies. It consists of \nall Regional Directors (RD) of the Ministry of Corporate Affairs as well as \nrepresentatives of the Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI) and \nthe Reserve Bank of India (RBI). Out of the companies that came out wi th  \nthe Initial Public Offers (IPO) during the years 1992 -2005, a total of 238 \ncompanies were identified as ‘Vanishing Companies’. Due to the continuous \nefforts of this Ministry, 129 companies have been traced and are now \nregular in filing statutory return s. Further, 32 vanishing companies were \nunder liquidation proceedings. After deletion of these 161(i.e. 129 plus 32) \ncompanies from the list of vanishing companies, the total number of \nVanishing Companies now stand at 77. \n(b) The Registrars of Companies ha ve initiated legal action in respect of \nthe Vanishing Companies, their Promoters and Directors. Besides carrying \nout physical verification of the Registered Offices, prosecutions have been \nlaunched for various offences under the Companies Act, 1956 as well  as \nunder the Indian Penal Code, 1860. \n******** ",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_170": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.170\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 29TH NOVEMBER, 2019\nAYUSHMAN BHARAT YOJANA\n†*170. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the number of beneficiaries under the Ayushman Bharat Yojana till date,\nStatewise;\n(b) the number of hospitals empanelled under the said Yojana, State-wise;\n(c) the number of ben eficiaries provided assistance under the Yojana in Rajasthan\nand Delhi during the last year along with the funds released and spent in this\nregard, State-wise;\n(d) whether the number of applicants under ‘Pradhan Mantri Sahayata Yojana ’ has\ndecreased after implementation of the Ayushman Bharat Yojana; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 170* FOR 29TH NOVEMBER, 2019\n(a) Ayushman Bharat Yojana comprises two components namely (i) Provision\nof Comprehensive Primary Healthcare  through Ayushman Bharat - Health and\nWellness Centers (AB- HWC), and (ii) Ayushman Bharat- Pradhan Mantri Jan\nArogya Yojana (AB- PMJAY).\nServices at Ayushman Bharat - Health & Wellness Centres (AB -HWCs) are\nfree and universal to all.\nState-wise total number of eligible beneficiaries and number of beneficia ries\nwho have availed the treatment under the AB-PMJAY are at Annexure I.\n(b) State -wise number of hospitals empanelled under AB - PMJAY are at\nAnnexure II.\n(c) Based on the proposals received from the States/ UTs, AB -HWC is being\nimplemented in all Sta tes/ UTs except UT of Delhi. Details of funds released\nunder AB-HWCs are at annexure III.\nThe Union Territory of Delhi is not implementing AB -PMJAY.  As per data\nshared by the State Health Agency number of hospital admissions under AB -\nPMJAY in Rajasthan till 25.11.2019 is 4,36,303. The scheme has been launched\nin Rajasthan on 1st September, 2019 and the State has not sought any release of\nfunds so far. State -wise details of funds released under AB -PMJAY are at\nAnnexure-IV.\n(d) & (e) Questions or informatio n pertaining to Prime Minister’s National\nRelief Fund are not admissible under Rule 41 (2) (viii) and 41 (2) (xvii) of the\nRules and Procedure and Conduct of Business in the Lok Sabha since the corpus\nof the fund is entirely constituted out of voluntary pu blic contributions and not\nfrom any allocation from the Consolidated Fund of India.\n\nAnnexure-I\nState-wise details for beneficiary families covered under AB-PMJAY as on 25.11.2019\nsince inception.\nState\nBeneficiary\nfamilies covered\n(in Lakhs) *\nNo of Hospital\nAdmissions\nAndaman And Nicobar Islands 0.21 43\nAndhra Pradesh 144 5,08,673\nArunachal Pradesh 0.9 1,359\nAssam 27 95,768\nBihar 109 1,24,353\nChandigarh 0.7 2,317\nChhattisgarh 41.5 6,94,964\nDadra and Nagar Haveli 0.7 22,291\nDaman and Diu 0.5 8,320\nGoa 0.4 10,064\nGujarat 70 10,27,720\nHaryana 15.5 65,889\nHimachal Pradesh 4.8 41,224\nUT Jammu And Kashmir and UT of Ladakh 6.1 43,748\nJharkhand 57 3,64,480\nKarnataka 115 4,08,788\nKerala 40.9 6,11,648\nLakshadweep 0.01 1\nMadhya Pradesh 118 2,10,190\nMaharashtra 83.6 2,45,386\nManipur 2.8 8,766\nMeghalaya 8.4 83,070\nMizoram 1.95 26,639\nNagaland 5.7 6,017\nNHCP - -\nPSU - -\nPuducherry 1 146\nPunjab 45.87 64,914\nRajasthan 59.7 4,36,303\nSikkim 0.4 685\nTamil Nadu 147 7,30,052\nTripura 4.9 43,102\nUttar Pradesh 118 2,40,526\nUttarakhand 19.7 1,04,013\nWest Bengal ** 111.9 17,636\nGRAND TOTAL 1,363.14 62,49,095\n* The above information relates to beneficiaries supported under PMJAY in alliance with state schemes\n** West Bengal has withdrawn implementation of scheme from January 2019.\n\nAnnexure – II\nNumber of Hospitals empanelled under the AB- PMJAY (as on 25.11.2019)\nState Total Hos