������������������������������ ������������������������������������������������ ������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������\n��\n��\n��",
    "ls|LS17_S2_U_88": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 88\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\nFACILITIES IN PRIMARY HEALTH CENTRES\n*88. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note of huge shortage of doctors in\nPublic/Primary Health Centres (PHCs) across the country, particularly in rural\nareas;\n(b) if so, the details the reof and the steps taken by the Government to meet the\nshortage of doctors and improve the doctor-patient ratio;\n(c) whether the Government proposes any scheme to upgrade the facilities at PHCs\nin the country and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether any survey has been conducted by the Government in this regard; and\n(e) if so, the details and the findings thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(DR. HARSH VARDHAN)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 88* FOR 22ND NOVEMBER, 2019\n(a) & (b) Public Health and Hospitals being a State Subject, all the administrative\nand personnel matters, including that of recruitment of doctors at Primary Health\nCentres (PHCs), lies with the State Governments. The shortage of doctors in\npublic health facilities varies from State to State depending upon their policies and\ncontext. However, as per Rural Health Statistics (RHS) – 2018, State/UT wise\ndetails of availability of doctors at PHCs and shortage thereof is given at\nAnnexure-I.\nUnder National Healt h Mission (NHM), financial and technical support is\nprovided to States/UTs to strengthen their healthcare systems including support for\nrecruitment of doctors on contractual basis, based on the requirements posed by\nthem in their Programme Implementation P lans (PIPs) within their overall\nresource envelope.\nIn order to meet the shortage of doctors in PHCs and to improve doctor -\npatient ratio, the States are encouraged to adopt flexible norms for engaging\ndoctors for public health facilities.  Financial support is also provided to States for\nproviding performan ce-based incentives, hard area allowance, providing\naccommodation and transport facilities in rural and remote areas, etc for engaging\ndoctors in the public health facilities. Also, States are advised to put in place\ntransparent policies of posting and tra nsfer, and ensure rational deployment of\nhealthcare professionals. State/UT Governments are impressed upon from time to\ntime to fill up the vacant posts including those of doctors.\nThe Ministry has also taken measures to improve the availability of doctors\nthrough establishment of new Government colleges and increase of seats to\nexisting Government medical colleges under centrally sponsored scheme and\nrationalization of norms for Government medical colleges in terms of faculty, land\n\netc. to facilitate setting up of new medical colleges. More than 29000 MBBS seats\nhave been increased in the last 5 years.\n(c) As per the budget announcement 2017 -18, 1.5 lakh Sub Health Centres and\nPrimary Health Centres are being transformed into Health and Well ness Centres\nacross the country under Ayushman Bharat (AB -HWCs) for provision of\ncomprehensive primary care that includes preventive healthcare and health\npromotion at the community level with continuum of care approach.\nThe AB-HWCs will provide a n expanded range of services to include care\nfor non - communicable diseases, palliative and rehabilitative care, Oral, Eye and\nENT care, mental health and first level care for emergencies and trauma as well as\nhealth promotion and wellness activities like Yoga apart from services already\nbeing provided for maternal and child health including immunization and\ncommunicable diseases.\n(d) & (e) Rural Health Statistics (RHS) is an annual publication, based on Health\ncare administrative data generated by the health care system. RHS provides\nreliable and updated information on rural health infrastructure and requirements of\npublic health facilities in rural areas. As per the RHS -2018, the state -wise details\nof shortfall of health facilities including PHCs, infrastructural facilities available\nand status of shortage/ vacancy position of human resources are at Annexure -II to\nAnnexure-VI.\n______\n\nANNEXURE-I\nDOCTORS+ AT PRIMARY HEALTH CENTRES (As on 31st March, 2018)\nS. No. State/UT Required1 Sanctioned In Position Vacant Shortfall\n[R] [S] [P] [S-P] [R-P]\n1 Andhra Pradesh 1147 2267 2045 222 *\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 143 NA 125 NA 18\n3 Assam 946 NA 1376 NA *\n4 Bihar # 1899 2078 1786 292 113\n5 Chhattisgarh 793 793 359 434 434\n6 Goa 25 48 56 * *\n7 Gujarat 1474 1865 1321 544 153\n8 Haryana 368 551 491 60 *\n9 Himachal Pradesh 576 636 622 14 *\n10 Jammu & Kashmir 637 1347 694 653 *\n11 Jharkhand 298 556 340 216 *\n12 Karnataka 2359 2359 2136 223 223\n13 Kerala 849 1120 1169 * *\n14 Madhya Pradesh 1171 1771 1112 659 59\n15 Maharashtra 1823 3009 2929 80 *\n16 Manipur 91 238 194 44 *\n17 Meghalaya ## 108 128 130 * *\n18 Mizoram ### 57 152 59 93 *\n19 Nagaland 126 108 118 * 8\n20 Odisha 1288 1326 917 409 371\n21 Punjab 432 593 480 113 *\n22 Rajasthan 2078 2751 2396 355 *\n23 Sikkim 24 NA 24 NA 0\n24 Tamil Nadu 1421 3136 2780 356 *\n25 Telangana 643 1254 1066 188 *\n26 Tripura 108 0 119 * *\n27 Uttarakhand 257 425 241 184 16\n28 Uttar Pradesh 3621 4509 1344 3165 2277\n29 West Bengal 913 1268 1016 252 *\n30 A& N Islands 22 42 34 8 *\n31 Chandigarh 0 0 0 0 0\n32 D & N Haveli 9 15 8 7 1\n33 Daman & Diu 4 5 4 1 0\n34 Delhi 5 21 22 * *\n35 Lakshadweep 4 8 8 0 *\n36 Puducherry 24 38 46 * *\nAll India2/ Total 25743 34417 27567 8572 3673\nNotes:\nSource : Rural Health Statistics-2018\n# Sanctioned data for year 2011 used , ## Sanctioned data for year 2015 used\n### Sanctioned data for year 2013-14 use, NA : Not available, + Allopathic Doctors